{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 22,
  "title": "Vegetables",
  "description": "Name the vegetables, and learn the two that break the ending rule.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Vegetable names are the same kind of noun as fruit names, with one trap: the two you meet first, la patata and el tomate, are the ones that do not follow the ending rule.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "The nouns",
        "body": "La patata, la cebolla, la zanahoria, la lechuga and la espinaca are feminine. El tomate, el pimiento, el guisante, el brócoli and el calabacín are masculine. El tomate is the one to remember: it ends in -o, so it is masculine, but la patata ends in -a and is still feminine. The ending is only a guide.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "la patata",
            "en": "the potato"
          },
          {
            "es": "el tomate",
            "en": "the tomato"
          },
          {
            "es": "la zanahoria",
            "en": "the carrot"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Plurals with a twist",
        "body": "The plural adds -s or -es as usual: las patatas, los tomates, las zanahorias. Two words lose their ending: el brócoli becomes los brócolis and el calabacín becomes los calabacines. In the kitchen you say verduras for all of them at once.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "las patatas",
            "en": "the potatoes"
          },
          {
            "es": "los tomates",
            "en": "the tomatoes"
          },
          {
            "es": "unas verduras",
            "en": "some vegetables"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
